{"id": "02217a97-d5fb-4902-a8c4-e8432d989514", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T16:13:38.271373", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "855d779c-141b-4006-8433-d0c4ad163ce2", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/quarot_llama2_experiment_design.md <<'MD'\n\n### 8.2 Decoding analysis\n\nDominant operation. At decoding, every forward step reuses all weights and reads the full KV-cache of length S (or B*S with cross-batch sharing). Roofline (Section 5.5): per-token memory traffic is dominated by weights for S up to a few thousand, then transitions to KV-bound for very long S. Hypothesis H2 predicts that W4A4K4V4 reduces per-token traffic by a factor of about 3.5-4x in the small-S, large-B regimes and less in the long-S, small-B regime.\n\nWhat the cells measure.\n- (a) Per-token latency vs S, parameterized by arm, at fixed B. At B = 1 the per-token memory traffic scales linearly with S, so the W4A4K4V4 line should have a shallower slope than the FP16 line, with a near-constant vertical offset determined by the weight-read savings.\n- (b) Per-token latency vs B, parameterized by arm, at fixed S. As B grows, weight traffic amortizes per request (not per token), so the latency-vs-B slope flattens for FP16; for W4A4K4V4 the slope is less steep because the starting weight traffic is already ~1/4.\n- (c) Throughput vs B; the decoding throughput ceiling per GPU is bandwidth divided by per-token bytes; the upper-bound numbers implied by the Section 5.5 table define a trajectory that each arm's throughput should track at large B.\n- (d) End-to-end latency at fixed output length 128 vs 512. Differences between arms in this metric combine a fixed startup cost (prefill, addressed in 8.1) with the per-token slopes above; reporting it isolates whether the per-token advantage dominates the total at long outputs.\n\nAttribution. The attribution script mirrors Section 8.1:\n- Weight-only W4 effect: (QuaRot-W4A16 - FP16); isolates the cost of 4-bit weights and online dequant in the absence of A4 (rotation present).\n- A4 + online-dequant effect: (E2E-W4A4 - QuaRot-W4A16).\n- K4V4 effect: (E2E-W4A4 - E2E-W4A4-KV16). For 7B/13B, where KV traffic is a meaningful fraction at long S, this is expected to be the largest per-cell difference in the W4A4 cell. For 70B, the same difference is expected to shrink by an order of magnitude or more because the K dimension is eight-fold smaller.\n- Rotation value with A4 active: (E2E-W4A4 - RTN-W4A4). This single comparison carries both the speed and the accuracy benefit of QuaRot over a naive int-only quantization.\n\nQuality pre-check before reporting decoding deltas: identical to Section 8.1, plus a paired comparison of E2E-W4A4-KV16 vs E2E-W4A4 to detect whether dropping KV4 (restoring FP16 KV) recovers quality; if so, the latency table explicitly flags the arm as a higher-quality mode.\n\nConditional 70B tier (GQA). GQA reduces KV bandwidth; the decoding analysis reuses the same plot axes. The expected signature is a smaller per-cell benefit of K4V4 vs 7B/13B and a smaller absolute KV share of per-token traffic. The 70B row in every decoding plot is therefore reported on its own axis or with a 70B-only inset, to keep the comparison with MHA models interpretable.\n\n### 8.3 Joint prefill + decoding view\n\nFor each (model, arm) pair, a summary figure reports:\n- TTFT vs S at B = 1.\n- Per-token latency vs S at B = 1 and B = 64.\n- Throughput at large B.\n- KV-cache peak memory at S = 4096.\n- PPL and zero-shot accuracy delta vs FP16.\n\nThe summary is repeated for 7B, 13B, and conditionally 70B; each block carries its own CI and a one-line classification (compute-bound vs bandwidth-bound) derived from the cell's empirical FLOPs/byte ratio. The figure is the unified reviewer-facing artifact of the eventual measurement phase; this design defines it but does not produce it.\n\n---\n\nEnd of design document. Implementation is not in scope; reviewers are invited to comment on the (a) workload grid, (b) the choice of K4V4 vs K4V16 as the single KV ablation, (c) the success criteria in Section 4, and (d) the threats listed in Section 7.2.\nMD\necho \"Final appended, $(wc -c < /workspace/quarot_llama2_experiment_design.md) bytes, $(wc -l < /workspace/quarot_llama2_experiment_design.md) lines\"\nls -la /workspace/", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-cff40e02-d4a3-42d7-b4e3-900444d53c55", "tool_call": {"id": "call-cff40e02-d4a3-42d7-b4e3-900444d53c55", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append Section 8.2 and 8.3, end of doc\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/quarot_llama2_experiment_design.md <<'MD'\\n\\n### 8.2 Decoding analysis\\n\\nDominant operation. At decoding, every forward step reuses all weights and reads the full KV-cache of length S (or B*S with cross-batch sharing). Roofline (Section 5.5): per-token memory traffic is dominated by weights for S up to a few thousand, then transitions to KV-bound for very long S. Hypothesis H2 predicts that W4A4K4V4 reduces per-token traffic by a factor of about 3.5-4x in the small-S, large-B regimes and less in the long-S, small-B regime.\\n\\nWhat the cells measure.\\n- (a) Per-token latency vs S, parameterized by arm, at fixed B. At B = 1 the per-token memory traffic scales linearly with S, so the W4A4K4V4 line should have a shallower slope than the FP16 line, with a near-constant vertical offset determined by the weight-read savings.\\n- (b) Per-token latency vs B, parameterized by arm, at fixed S. As B grows, weight traffic amortizes per request (not per token), so the latency-vs-B slope flattens for FP16; for W4A4K4V4 the slope is less steep because the starting weight traffic is already ~1/4.\\n- (c) Throughput vs B; the decoding throughput ceiling per GPU is bandwidth divided by per-token bytes; the upper-bound numbers implied by the Section 5.5 table define a trajectory that each arm's throughput should track at large B.\\n- (d) End-to-end latency at fixed output length 128 vs 512. Differences between arms in this metric combine a fixed startup cost (prefill, addressed in 8.1) with the per-token slopes above; reporting it isolates whether the per-token advantage dominates the total at long outputs.\\n\\nAttribution. The attribution script mirrors Section 8.1:\\n- Weight-only W4 effect: (QuaRot-W4A16 - FP16); isolates the cost of 4-bit weights and online dequant in the absence of A4 (rotation present).\\n- A4 + online-dequant effect: (E2E-W4A4 - QuaRot-W4A16).\\n- K4V4 effect: (E2E-W4A4 - E2E-W4A4-KV16). For 7B/13B, where KV traffic is a meaningful fraction at long S, this is expected to be the largest per-cell difference in the W4A4 cell. For 70B, the same difference is expected to shrink by an order of magnitude or more because the K dimension is eight-fold smaller.\\n- Rotation value with A4 active: (E2E-W4A4 - RTN-W4A4). This single comparison carries both the speed and the accuracy benefit of QuaRot over a naive int-only quantization.\\n\\nQuality pre-check before reporting decoding deltas: identical to Section 8.1, plus a paired comparison of E2E-W4A4-KV16 vs E2E-W4A4 to detect whether dropping KV4 (restoring FP16 KV) recovers quality; if so, the latency table explicitly flags the arm as a higher-quality mode.\\n\\nConditional 70B tier (GQA). GQA reduces KV bandwidth; the decoding analysis reuses the same plot axes. The expected signature is a smaller per-cell benefit of K4V4 vs 7B/13B and a smaller absolute KV share of per-token traffic. The 70B row in every decoding plot is therefore reported on its own axis or with a 70B-only inset, to keep the comparison with MHA models interpretable.\\n\\n### 8.3 Joint prefill + decoding view\\n\\nFor each (model, arm) pair, a summary figure reports:\\n- TTFT vs S at B = 1.\\n- Per-token latency vs S at B = 1 and B = 64.\\n- Throughput at large B.\\n- KV-cache peak memory at S = 4096.\\n- PPL and zero-shot accuracy delta vs FP16.\\n\\nThe